[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-160481"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/b8874b14fa80881e69cb1daee180d2e342a27c5902e66fc75d43622278176b09.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/fcfc165548d8174ec0468dce53317fd0c8daa14565852c5e44d6764161723bed.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{2},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-160910/selection/cbe3ba357477746a968d758f16339dde5b1002c0be6e73e7e275dcf2e8e3a6cf" :answer/id #uuid "182a87fe-7576-540f-9c89-fad5af46efdc" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/2fbee04fdedee3c2ef0b0f0da2022909ff8d9c2bea7e16b158517afe8fc35c43.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160910/selection/ed655e2f0cc2f7eb9ca477373c98a2cf5013c05a17e3488a0bcdf260ba32d831" :answer/id #uuid "8e3eebcf-491a-5a43-b821-1fa9090c6527" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/a31fe0c820f7d69c3a6c7bb64c3deac3b0be917f1edea273feac2353edb2df58.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160910/selection/7c9afe8b3b98df75f0a8cce8fa925cd756131dd8096d1d0b7bb00d1cd8665031" :answer/id #uuid "d167ecc3-fe70-56bb-8fe9-057856018fcf" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/d951dd353ee85920ecaf73724defb0f8a9f902810f3ce333da54fee96ca2eba1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160910/selection/c40358ac5f1654e6febb3e78727a18a20382faf6def069b6cd5ddf3d9dd2dd21" :answer/id #uuid "9a747a82-34d2-5656-b099-b69376c87d8b" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/5bb9d3386eab3293e8319d7604b13e4a530f8311610ba8f0aba8f70e11aa32b0.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160910/selection/922dd2ab28e0797cf2833402d0a03f659abd833af4d3b10695a7e23c07cfb7f9" :answer/id #uuid "88dacfce-cca7-5a66-b7bc-dde2266c0439" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/f8e51d08054d568fc7caad893e7c82421e032c45b962399fd02d5c90bbe83e7f.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160910/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "49bc7734-f858-595d-b174-536641b696a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-160910/selection/cbe3ba357477746a968d758f16339dde5b1002c0be6e73e7e275dcf2e8e3a6cf" :answer-field/choices ["q-160910/selection/cbe3ba357477746a968d758f16339dde5b1002c0be6e73e7e275dcf2e8e3a6cf" "q-160910/selection/ed655e2f0cc2f7eb9ca477373c98a2cf5013c05a17e3488a0bcdf260ba32d831" "q-160910/selection/7c9afe8b3b98df75f0a8cce8fa925cd756131dd8096d1d0b7bb00d1cd8665031" "q-160910/selection/c40358ac5f1654e6febb3e78727a18a20382faf6def069b6cd5ddf3d9dd2dd21" "q-160910/selection/922dd2ab28e0797cf2833402d0a03f659abd833af4d3b10695a7e23c07cfb7f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-160910" :question/id #uuid "36f830e5-a4bd-5767-8e9d-391ce75da407" :question/math-academy-id "q-160910" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/ad0e3ddbeb228e3fdeeb09823493fb54a4a7f930c25ecd3b69b71dcf77e1d923.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{7}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{7})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{7}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{7}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{7}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{7}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{7},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/2fbee04fdedee3c2ef0b0f0da2022909ff8d9c2bea7e16b158517afe8fc35c43.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-160910/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160910"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-160928/selection/83b026997d77eb1efce3bdf036cbf2c3e4cb9ca932f79496e854c81994dbc28f" :answer/id #uuid "36ed1b21-9c88-5a15-a42e-8fa36b4356a6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/10a9d70717e657082fd8c36dc98876fcea53297ce79b44585574efef821dba51.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160928/selection/11cdc4aa223c6df14fce35e7a270fa5178ca3a99fe174d8591c8036feff4948b" :answer/id #uuid "14f9ff73-afc2-5947-ab98-ae4133fed0df" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/90571cebb5f46697204869a7dbd07e83036d4fe92a5f46045bf6279b3b307999.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160928/selection/2d3ac2263d90d648f7e9ea35f118da5eeb94cf7e99d07918ef1be93ce3ee12d9" :answer/id #uuid "a6ef46f8-9615-5c8b-96bb-0bf317ed3b86" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/e718cdc16345d0da40874901ed4dc276c5cf33a75949365b59129a7bb934a8c1.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160928/selection/c8a35532dbbfec998c0da7de976f0ad9ab1d72539a1a3aa6cb1e6b1f08fba54e" :answer/id #uuid "db9b7b8f-6de0-50a2-b912-ca7a8a8000e6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/9d781ed8813991d36d3da4802efa94543f1ee3fc542e1b8c72ff7ad521974e58.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160928/selection/fccbb993eca25a5f109acb26d582a918f0f778d47cc42543756d2dc33ebf85b5" :answer/id #uuid "4e935985-d45b-51e1-8d65-92a5a357a0b9" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/8e3b059ba260063bed9924a2d9ebf2894d848b2a38781cf45e1ce7a365562e6d.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-160928/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "eef60b9a-278e-5798-b841-d7bb98c5c856" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-160928/selection/fccbb993eca25a5f109acb26d582a918f0f778d47cc42543756d2dc33ebf85b5" :answer-field/choices ["q-160928/selection/83b026997d77eb1efce3bdf036cbf2c3e4cb9ca932f79496e854c81994dbc28f" "q-160928/selection/11cdc4aa223c6df14fce35e7a270fa5178ca3a99fe174d8591c8036feff4948b" "q-160928/selection/2d3ac2263d90d648f7e9ea35f118da5eeb94cf7e99d07918ef1be93ce3ee12d9" "q-160928/selection/c8a35532dbbfec998c0da7de976f0ad9ab1d72539a1a3aa6cb1e6b1f08fba54e" "q-160928/selection/fccbb993eca25a5f109acb26d582a918f0f778d47cc42543756d2dc33ebf85b5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-160928" :question/id #uuid "0aaf351a-049f-501d-8313-1944db65783b" :question/math-academy-id "q-160928" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/85e8422a0b3421db73d8cbe88ef3c51b59f628f233d48982840a21c4d26bd26f.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=4$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=4$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=4,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/8e3b059ba260063bed9924a2d9ebf2894d848b2a38781cf45e1ce7a365562e6d.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-160928/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-160928"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161032/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "50ed8644-7bf8-5648-bd64-596232dee69e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161032/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "7296e9f9-a582-5d00-bc5c-f15a143c4dbe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161032/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "c567f911-29ad-56df-9c93-1830920118bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161032/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "246f6107-5c01-5099-83ed-824fc696247e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161032/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "8f3c907e-9c7c-5d39-948a-d987041dd6fa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161032/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c73baaa8-8f23-5b02-ab9f-485244a420b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161032/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-161032/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-161032/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-161032/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-161032/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-161032/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161032" :question/id #uuid "4fec2c47-e34e-5493-9239-cfee567a5c49" :question/math-academy-id "q-161032" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/16d74107dbbf12749766cd9cea7e38d16e28a59efb997bd224aed73c7830324e.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{10\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{10\\pi }{9}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The image makes the angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n- The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and $2\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),\\,2\\le u\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/bfe75ef6b4ea9ddd72be6501f95fd89dd1e80fb2aed691c3c22b158514141cc1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=2,$ we obtain the point \n$(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $2.$\n\n- When $u=4,$ we obtain the point \n$(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $4.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-161032/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161032"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-161108/selection/f31876a3b57f79e4a228e5d96f5783dc216d3336f1a8f65c3d947440f755d00a" :answer/id #uuid "921c1f31-9457-5164-b9fc-846777defa18" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/72c832e8bd3990885826013f8ab49e5e50bd6ac3bb567b999e0a8a2bbdf5f346.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161108/selection/55fa90e4e13eadb6e6688aa358cd392dfb86716c36817c26248cc0c0480a181c" :answer/id #uuid "f084828a-15e0-5110-8822-7d37f6c72031" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/ab94778cea0a627b227e39745bc9efe3f516b5bcfb5560ee70d6fd30fa58289a.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161108/selection/d5fb1f3a3caf39e9ce863bd8062d4aa40b5eaf978a359c066d50f0258ee58f31" :answer/id #uuid "bd8c6524-2138-5fc3-b4ce-d0ac039c26a2" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/2804981f7e56212c632bab38ff49f7b1a641f708b69dbaa1228cccd82c9865c7.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161108/selection/79ba4b76900ecd7867b11fde687b6d595ac069cf0976f63bf824c9fade11dce8" :answer/id #uuid "ef333a41-7c55-5af4-8d82-97899f213a28" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/41c26aac58a4c8c2b577f9d3d17c90bd6cbd6971709c42f8a74117db180da899.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161108/selection/f948ca51b1b43e89ab5b50c7053fbadee0d42dcd9c36599355ec1e948d733b0b" :answer/id #uuid "95b468e1-8c72-507b-bf2d-98009500f8b6" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/4a10a6686caaf68c7986ce9c4b60f78f1a21c6414b02133e6c9c82461e1ad33b.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-161108/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d9cb0a88-4ba9-5308-bc56-858c19707918" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-161108/selection/55fa90e4e13eadb6e6688aa358cd392dfb86716c36817c26248cc0c0480a181c" :answer-field/choices ["q-161108/selection/f31876a3b57f79e4a228e5d96f5783dc216d3336f1a8f65c3d947440f755d00a" "q-161108/selection/55fa90e4e13eadb6e6688aa358cd392dfb86716c36817c26248cc0c0480a181c" "q-161108/selection/d5fb1f3a3caf39e9ce863bd8062d4aa40b5eaf978a359c066d50f0258ee58f31" "q-161108/selection/79ba4b76900ecd7867b11fde687b6d595ac069cf0976f63bf824c9fade11dce8" "q-161108/selection/f948ca51b1b43e89ab5b50c7053fbadee0d42dcd9c36599355ec1e948d733b0b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-161108" :question/id #uuid "a237fc30-2635-5842-8b88-754529781a94" :question/math-academy-id "q-161108" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/aa8020019d441b741a9b621a0015fda31b3aa199afd8fc145aa840b1749e7a8b.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $u=2,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071828/assets/ab94778cea0a627b227e39745bc9efe3f516b5bcfb5560ee70d6fd30fa58289a.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-161108/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-161108"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
